---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Navigation } from '@/components/Navigation';
import { Footer } from '@/components/Footer';
import { ConsentBanner } from '@/components/ConsentBanner';
---

<BaseLayout 
  title="What to Expect from a Thorough Assessment – RMT North Vancouver | Scandinavian Clinic"
  description="What a thorough assessment for muscle and joint pain includes — history, movement testing, segment-by-segment joint testing, and muscle evaluation — and how it works at Scandinavian Clinic, North Vancouver."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen bg-background">
    <Navigation client:load />
    
    <main class="pt-24 pb-16">
      <div class="container mx-auto px-4 max-w-4xl">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What to Expect from a Thorough Assessment</h1>
        
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-muted-foreground">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">What does a thorough assessment involve?</h2>
          <p class="text-xl font-medium text-foreground mb-8 border-l-4 border-primary pl-4">
            A thorough assessment has four parts: a detailed history, an examination of how you move — where range is lost and where your left and right sides no longer match, testing of the individual joints and spinal segments, and evaluation of your muscles as a working system. Asymmetry in movement is a key indicator as is an abnormal range of motion. The findings, not assumptions, guide the treatment.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">How do you know whether a therapist will really assess you?</h2>
          <p>I can only tell you what I do and why. My assessment-based treatments begins with your pain but also the question of <em>why</em> it hurts.</p>
          <p>There are practical signs you can look for, whoever you're considering. Does the first visit begin with a structured history and physical examination before any hands-on treatment? Are you asked to bring clothing that allows your spine and joints to be observed? Are specific tests performed — and are the findings explained to you in plain language before treatment starts? Does the treatment plan follow from those findings?</p>
          <p>This page describes what each of those steps involves and what they are designed to find, so you know what to expect — and what to ask — wherever you seek care.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">Step 1: What happens during the clinical history?</h2>
          <p>Your first visit begins with a conversation, not a table. Eva takes a detailed history: when the symptoms started, what movements or situations trigger them, what makes them better or worse, how they affect your daily function, and what has already been tried. Previous imaging (X-ray, MRI) and medical records add useful context if you have them.</p>
          <p>The history does two jobs. It narrows down the likely mechanical sources of the problem before the physical examination begins, and it establishes your goals — because treatment for a trail runner with recurring hip pain is planned differently from treatment for someone who can't sit through a workday.</p>
          <p>Many patients arrive having tried other approaches without lasting results. A careful history often surfaces patterns — how the pain returns, what it responds to — that often point toward factors not previously identified.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">Step 2: What does the physical examination involve?</h2>
          <p>After the history, Eva examines how you actually move, in detail. The body is built to move symmetrically and efficiently, and it works as one connected system — joints, muscles, and the movement patterns that coordinate them. When one side moves less than the other, when a segment is restricted, or when a muscle group is doing work its counterpart should share, the body compensates. Those compensations — asymmetries and departures from efficient movement — are the key indicators the examination looks for, because they point to where the problem is being driven from, not just where it is felt. This functional, whole-system view — treating the movement pattern rather than only the painful spot — is the tradition of Czech physicians Karel Lewit and Vladimir Janda (with whom I have completed several courses).</p>
          <p>The examination reads three connected layers together — movement, joints, and muscles. No single layer gives the answer on its own; the picture comes from how the findings line up.</p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">How is movement tested?</h3>
          
          <div class="my-8 aspect-video bg-muted flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg border-2 border-dashed border-border">
            <span class="text-muted-foreground font-medium">[VIDEO 1 PLACEHOLDER: Movement testing]</span>
          </div>

          <p>Movement testing looks at how you move as a whole. Eva observes and measures your active movement — bending, turning, reaching — and reads it against two references: the range that movement should have, and the symmetry between your left and right sides.</p>
          <p>Asymmetry is the key indicator here. A movement that is restricted or uneven side-to-side shows where the body has adapted or compensated, often well away from the spot that hurts. And a movement that reproduces your familiar pain shows which structures are actually involved — a controlled way of letting the pain point to its own source.</p>
          <p>This is why movement testing comes first. It tells Eva where to look more closely with the joint and muscle testing that follows.</p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">How are joints tested? The spine as an example.</h3>
          
          <div class="my-8 aspect-video bg-muted flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg border-2 border-dashed border-border">
            <span class="text-muted-foreground font-medium">[VIDEO 2 PLACEHOLDER: Segmental joint testing]</span>
          </div>

          <p>The spine is not one joint but a chain of segments, and each segment contributes its own share of movement. Eva tests the segments individually — how each one moves, and whether it moves the same to the left as to the right.</p>
          <p>A segment that moves freely one way but is restricted the other is an imbalance, and it is treatable, because the restriction sits in the tissues around the segment: the joint itself, the ligaments, and the muscles that control it. Karel Lewit described how these local restrictions link into chain reactions through the locomotor system — one reason a restricted segment can show up far from where it is felt. This is detail that whole-body movement testing can't reach. Two people with the same back pain can have restrictions at completely different segments — which is why the same treatment can help one and do little for the other.</p>

          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mt-8 mb-4">How are muscles tested?</h3>
          
          <div class="my-8 aspect-video bg-muted flex items-center justify-center rounded-lg border-2 border-dashed border-border">
            <span class="text-muted-foreground font-medium">[VIDEO 3 PLACEHOLDER: Muscle system evaluation]</span>
          </div>

          <p>Muscles are tested as a system, alongside the movement and joint findings — not as isolated parts. Eva assesses tension, tightness, and the balance between muscle groups: which muscles are overworking, which are underactive, and how those patterns fit the restrictions found in the earlier tests. The tendency for certain muscles to tighten while their opposites weaken — in predictable patterns — was mapped by neurologist Vladimir Janda.</p>
          <p>This matters because the muscle findings direct the treatment. A muscle held in chronic tension needs release or stretching; its underactive counterpart needs activation and training. Treating one without the other tends to give short-lived results, because the imbalance driving the tension is still there.</p>
          <p>Read together, the three layers complete the picture: where the problem shows up, which segments are involved, and which muscular imbalances are holding it in place.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">Step 3: How does treatment begin?</h2>
          <p>Treatment follows the findings — and it's often gradual. Once the assessment is complete, Eva explains what was found in plain language, then begins treatment in the same visit where time allows. Techniques are chosen to match the findings: massage and soft-tissue work including fascia, joint mobilization for restricted segments, specific stretching, and neuromuscular techniques to restore normal movement control.</p>
          <p>Because the body adapts progressively, treatment is planned the same way. Some acute problems resolve in two to three sessions. Chronic imbalances that have built up over years are typically corrected step by step, with each session building on the changes from the last. Mild soreness for 24–48 hours after treatment is common as the body adjusts.</p>
          <p>Eva will discuss a realistic plan of care with you based on the findings and your goals — including, where relevant, periodic maintenance.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">Step 4: What about training and preventing it from coming back?</h2>
          <p>Lasting results depend on what happens between sessions. As treatment restores normal movement and balance, Eva adds training and instructions to keep it: home exercises matched to your specific findings, with instruction on how to perform them correctly, and practical guidance on the positions and habits that fed the problem in the first place.</p>
          <p>For many patients this includes elements of Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilization (DNS) — an active approach to training the deep stabilizing system of the trunk: the diaphragm, pelvic floor, and abdominal wall working together. DNS was developed from the developmental-kinesiology work of Pavel Kolář and colleagues at the Prague School of Rehabilitation.</p>

          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-foreground mt-12 mb-4">What should you bring, and how long is the first visit?</h2>
          <p>Book 60 minutes for your initial visit if you can so there is time for both the full assessment and treatment (45 minutes is also workable). Please arrive 10 minutes early to complete the intake form covering your medical history and current symptoms.</p>
          <p>Bring a tank top and shorts — the examination depends on being able to observe your spine, posture, and joint movement directly, while keeping you comfortable. If you have previous imaging (X-rays, MRI) or diagnostic reports, bring them; they're not required, but they add valuable context.</p>

          <div class="bg-card p-6 rounded-xl border border-border shadow-sm my-12">
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-foreground mb-4">Assessment at Scandinavian Clinic, North Vancouver</h3>
            <p class="mb-6">Eva Andersson trained in Orthopedic Manual Therapy in Sweden through a five-year post-secondary clinical program, and in Dynamic Neuromuscular Stabilization in the Czech Republic. Every treatment at Scandinavian Clinic begins with the assessment described on this page — a full biomechanical evaluation, not assumptions about where it hurts.</p>
            <a href="#" class="inline-block bg-accent hover:bg-accent/90 text-accent-foreground font-medium py-3 px-6 rounded-md transition-colors">Book your initial assessment →</a>
            <p class="text-sm mt-4">Suite 202 – 101 West 16th Street, North Vancouver · (604) 926-4883</p>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </main>
    
    <Footer client:idle />
    <ConsentBanner client:load />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose p { margin-bottom: 1.5em; line-height: 1.75; }
</style>
